    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Load data\n",
        "The raster data in our current `item` is in the `image` asset. \n",
        "Again, we access this data via its URL. \n",
        "This time, we open it using `rioxr.open_rasterio()` directly:"
      ],
      "id": "d29867c4"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "sb = rioxr.open_rasterio(item.assets['image'].href)\n",
        "sb"
      ],
      "id": "f686a694",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Notice the output doesn't show any data values, only `[526656000 values with dtype=uint8]`.\n",
        "That's because `open_rasterio()` only read the file's metadata: its dimensions, coordinates and CRS.\n",
        "The data itself (about 500 MB) stays in the cloud until we use it, for example to plot it or to compute a statistic.\n",
        "This is the same lazy behavior we saw with generators.\n",
        "The NAIP files are stored as **Cloud-Optimized GeoTIFFs (COGs)**, a format designed so we can read just the part of a file we need instead of downloading all of it.\n",
        "\n",
        "Notice this raster has four bands (red, green, blue, nir), so we cannot plot it directly: `.plot.imshow()` would read the fourth band (NIR) as transparency. \n",
        "Thus we need to select the bands we want to plot (RGB) before plotting:"
      ],
      "id": "efa20056"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: false\n",
        "# Plot raster with correct ratio\n",
        "size = 6  \n",
        "aspect = sb.rio.width / sb.rio.height \n",
        "# Select R,G,B bands and plot\n",
        "sb.sel(band=[1,2,3]).plot.imshow(size=size, aspect=aspect)"
      ],
      "id": "f6ae8de0",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
